explanation:
This complete example uses two interchangeable tools through one protocol.

```swift
protocol DrawingTool {
    var description: String { get }
}
struct Pencil: DrawingTool {
    var description: String { "Pencil" }
}
struct Brush: DrawingTool {
    var description: String { "Brush" }
}
func toolMessage(using tool: any DrawingTool) -> String {
    "Today I will use a \(tool.description)."
}
print(toolMessage(using: Pencil()))
print(toolMessage(using: Brush()))
```

The colon after each struct name announces conformance. any DrawingTool means a value of some type that meets DrawingTool's requirements. The function can read description on either type. This needs a Swift compiler that supports the any syntax.

