explanation:
This complete Swift program encodes and decodes entries in memory using Foundation, Apple's general-purpose library module.

```swift
import Foundation

struct ReadingEntry: Codable, Equatable {
    let title: String
    let minutes: Int
}

let original = [ReadingEntry(title: "History", minutes: 15)]
do {
    let bytes = try JSONEncoder().encode(original)
    let restored = try JSONDecoder().decode([ReadingEntry].self, from: bytes)
    print(restored == original)
} catch {
    print("Entry data could not be read or written")
}
```

The usual result is true. Codable supplies encoding and decoding abilities for this model. Equatable allows equality comparisons. JSON is a text-based data format; encoded Data stores bytes. This example checks an in-memory round trip, not disk persistence.

This separate complete Swift program writes valid JSON to a temporary file, reads it back, and handles failure.

```swift
import Foundation

struct ReadingEntry: Codable, Equatable {
    let title: String
    let minutes: Int
}

let entries = [ReadingEntry(title: "Geography", minutes: 25)]
let url = FileManager.default.temporaryDirectory
    .appendingPathComponent("reading-example.json")

do {
    let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(entries)
    try data.write(to: url, options: .atomic)
    let storedData = try Data(contentsOf: url)
    let restored = try JSONDecoder().decode([ReadingEntry].self, from: storedData)
    print(restored == entries)
    print(FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: url.path))
} catch {
    print("Saved entries could not be read or written")
}
```

On success, both outputs are true. URL describes a file location. appendingPathComponent adds a filename to the directory URL. path gives a filesystem path for the existence check. This example writes a real temporary file.

Overloading means functions can share a name when their input signatures differ. loadEntries(_ data: Data) loads supplied bytes; loadEntries(from url: URL) reads the file and then reuses the byte decoder. The underscore lets the Data call omit a label, while from is the URL call's label. Data("broken".utf8) makes bytes from the letters of broken using UTF-8 text encoding. Those bytes are not valid JSON for [PracticeEntry], so they should exercise the catch path. A file-read failure and a decode failure are different causes, but both need an understandable outcome.

This complete local example calls both actual overloads:
```swift
import Foundation
struct PracticeEntry: Codable { let title: String; let minutes: Int }
struct LoadResult { let entries: [PracticeEntry]; let message: String }
func loadEntries(_ data: Data) -> LoadResult {
    do {
        let entries = try JSONDecoder().decode([PracticeEntry].self, from: data)
        return LoadResult(entries: entries, message: "Loaded")
    } catch {
        return LoadResult(entries: [], message: "Data could not be read")
    }
}
func loadEntries(from url: URL) -> LoadResult {
    do { return loadEntries(try Data(contentsOf: url)) }
    catch { return LoadResult(entries: [], message: "File could not be read") }
}
let bytes = Data("[{\"title\":\"Map\",\"minutes\":5}]".utf8)
let url = FileManager.default.temporaryDirectory.appendingPathComponent("worked-overloads.json")
try bytes.write(to: url, options: .atomic)
print(loadEntries(bytes).entries.count)
print(loadEntries(from: url).entries.count)
print(loadEntries(Data("broken".utf8)).entries.count)
```
The outputs are 1, 1, 0. The unlabeled call receives bytes; the from-labeled call receives a file URL and delegates after reading. Both return LoadResult with entries and a message rather than throwing a read/decode error to the caller. The separate write can still throw. This local example writes a temporary file; it is not a claim of browser filesystem access.

